VERDICT
======================================================================
Median mean-Ce   : pinned-low 0.0763 | pinned-high 0.6050 | interior 0.7871
Median mean-SAS  : pinned-low 3.917 | pinned-high 3.667 | interior 3.822

EXPOSURE HYPOTHESIS: SUPPORTED for pinned-low. Those patients have
   materially lower effect-site concentration than interior patients,
   10.3x lower at the median, so the sigmoid has
   little leverage and the objective runs monotonically to a bound.

SECOND MECHANISM: the pinned-high group is a different failure entirely.
   All 45 pinned-high patients are EXACTLY the patients whose
   calibration window contributed zero usable points, and no others.
   With no points to accumulate, ec50_loss returns loss/max(n,1) = 0
   for every candidate, so the objective is identically flat and
   minimize_scalar's bounded search terminates at ~9.999995 by
   construction. Their EC50 is not an estimate of anything: it is
   the optimiser's terminus on an empty objective. Note their drug
   exposure is NORMAL (median mean-Ce 0.6050 vs interior 0.7871),
   so the exposure hypothesis does NOT explain this group.

SAS DIRECTION CHECK (the brief expected pinned-low awake / pinned-high sedated):
   mean SAS: pinned-low 3.92 | pinned-high 3.67 | interior 3.82
   spread across groups is 0.25 SAS units
   NOT SUPPORTED. The groups barely differ in sedation level, so
   pinning is not tracking how awake or sedated a patient is.
   The comparison that matters is against the model's own drug-free
   baseline E0 = 7. Every group sits far below it:
      pinned-low   mean SAS 3.51  ->  3.49 units below E0
      pinned-high  mean SAS 3.45  ->  3.55 units below E0
      interior     mean SAS 3.70  ->  3.30 units below E0
   That gap, not the between-group difference, is what the optimiser is
   trying to close. A patient sitting ~3.4 SAS units below the drug-free
   baseline with almost no modelled drug on board leaves the loss
   monotonically decreasing in EC50, so it rails to the floor and still
   cannot reach the observation. The pinning is the calibration absorbing
   a baseline mismatch between the Eleveld E0/Emax scale and ICU SAS,
   not a pharmacological property of the patient.

IMPACT ON PREDICTION:
   pinned-low median MAE 0.2125 (n=165) vs interior 0.3220 (n=122), Mann-Whitney p = 0.0378
   Pinned-low patients are predicted MORE accurately than interior
   ones, not less. The degenerate EC50 is not hurting anything.
   The likely reason is confounding rather than benefit: pinned-low
   patients are the low-exposure ones, whose SAS moves least, and
   per-patient error tracks within-patient SAS variability almost
   perfectly (rho = 0.85, see instructions.txt Section 9). They are
   simply the easy patients. Two implications, both worth stating:
   the boundary artefact costs no accuracy, and a feature whose
   degeneracy costs no accuracy is a feature carrying little signal.

